  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Is the sum of two surjective functions from $\\mathbb{R}$ to $\\mathbb{R}$ necessarily surjective? Either prove this is the case or provide a counterexample.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No. For example, $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=-x$ are both surjective, but $(f+g)(x)=0$ has range $\\{0\\}$.",
      "worked": "Let $f,g:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be defined by $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=-x$. For every $y\\in\\mathbb{R}$, $f(y)=y$ and $g(-y)=y$, so both functions are surjective.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(f+g)(x)&=x+(-x)\\\\(f+g)(x)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nTheir sum has range $\\{0\\}$, so it is not surjective onto $\\mathbb{R}$. Therefore, the sum need not be surjective.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 38
  },
